Counterargument: this pilot tests the most dangerous interpretation of the changelog item in the worst host process.

The premise is weak: `mopsus` is a long-lived Telegram daemon, while interactive MCP auth is fundamentally an operator-local, foreground workflow. “Prime auth before bot starts” dodges the actual new capability; “fail closed if missing” means the pilot mostly tests env-flag plumbing, not interactive auth. Worse, `/debate <topic>` is user-prompt-shaped input, so attaching authenticated context there creates a clean path from Telegram text to private data access.

The proposed blackboard case is also muddled. If the MCP source is the read-only blackboard, why does it need OAuth? If it is the data room, the blast radius is much larger than “context-bearing debate”: it becomes an authenticated personal-data oracle inside an adversarial debate loop.

Failure modes are underweighted:

- OAuth prompt appears in a daemon subprocess and hangs.
- Auth URL or token-adjacent output leaks into Telegram logs.
- Prompt injection in `/debate` causes over-broad retrieval from authenticated context.
- Scheduled and manual paths accidentally share the same Codex invocation wrapper.
- “Read-only” still leaks sensitive data; mutability is not the only risk.

